<reference_puzzle index="1" source="train" puzzle_id="9f8de559" lines="19-37">
<description>
Erase the first border pixel hit by extending the red marker away from its pink trail.

Input structure
- The dominant background surrounds a unique red marker and a four-pixel
  pink trail.
- The pink pixels form one straight 8-connected ray starting next to the
  red marker.
- In the opposite direction from that trail, the red marker faces a
  non-background border or obstacle pixel beyond background cells.

Rule
- Keep the whole grid fixed except for the first non-background pixel on
  the ray from the red marker away from the pink trail.
- Recolor that one hit pixel to the background color.

Key insight
- The pink trail marks where the red marker came from, so the edit is found
  by ray-casting from the red marker in the opposite direction.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="2de01db2" lines="28-41">
<description>
Invert each row's native foreground color against the black background.

Input structure
- A black-background grid whose rows each have one native foreground color plus optional distractor colors.
- The native row color is the most frequent non-bg color in that row; if tied, its run starts leftmost among tied colors.

Rule
- For each row independently, turn pixels of the native row color into bg.
- Turn every other pixel in that row, including bg and distractor colors, into the native row color.

Key insight
- Distractor colors are not preserved objects; they mark cells that participate in the binary complement of the row's native color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="1be83260" lines="41-58">
<description>
Decode the colored slot map inside the marked motif and rebuild the motif lattice with those colors.

Input structure:
- A blank background surrounds a rectangular lattice of identical motifs.
- The motif rows and columns are separated by one background line.
- One motif has colored marker pixels in its internal odd-row/odd-column slots.

Rule:
- The motif size is 2R+1 by 2C+1, where R by C is the motif lattice size.
- Read the marked motif's odd internal slots as the color map for the R by C output motifs.
- Slots that still contain the repeated motif color map to that repeated motif color.
- Rebuild the compact lattice on a canvas filled with the repeated motif color,
  painting each motif mask with its decoded color.

Key insight:
- The holes and filled odd slots inside one motif are a miniature legend for recoloring the entire repeated motif lattice.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="f0100645" lines="51-68">
<description>
Slide each inner colored object horizontally into the same-colored vertical wall until it touches an obstacle.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- The only foreground colors are two non-background colors.
- One foreground color fills the left column and the other fills the right column.
- Extra 8-connected objects of each wall color appear away from that color's wall.

Rule
- Clear the extra objects from their original positions.
- For each wall color, process its 8-connected extra objects from nearest to farthest from the matching wall.
- Slide each object horizontally toward its matching wall as far as possible without overlapping the wall or an already settled object of the same color.
- Keep the two vertical wall columns fixed.

Key insight
- The side columns act like color-specific gravity walls: same-colored objects compact sideways against their own wall while preserving each object's shape.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="eval" puzzle_id="53fb4810" lines="105-119">
<description>
Extend marker tiles outward from '+' shapes by repeating them to the grid edge.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- A dominant non-bg color forms several '+'-shaped objects.
- Small colored patches (1–few cells) sit adjacent to each arm of a '+'.

Rule:
- For each marker tile touching a '+', determine which side of the '+' it sits on.
- Project the tile away from the '+' in that direction, repeating the tile pattern periodically until the grid boundary.
- '+' pixels are never overwritten.

Key insight: Each marker's attachment side determines its extension direction; the pattern repeats periodically, not as a single copy.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="230f2e48" lines="19-38">
<description>
Fold each straight red rod at its black marker, leaving the gray-anchored side fixed.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- Each foreground object is a straight horizontal or vertical 4-connected rod.
- Each rod contains many pixels of one line color, one endpoint marker, and one
  interior control marker.

Rule:
- For every rod, keep the segment from the endpoint marker through the control
  marker unchanged.
- Move the line-color tail on the far side of the control marker to a
  perpendicular ray starting next to that marker.
- Choose the perpendicular direction with more space to the grid edge.

Key insight:
- The endpoint marker identifies the fixed side of the rod, while the control
  marker is the hinge; the tail folds into the larger open half-plane.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="eval" puzzle_id="2b83f449" lines="10-22">
<description>
Simulate tokens falling through a pipe network after gates rotate open.

Input structure:
- Wall (0), bg (pipe fill), gate (horizontal 1×3 bars), token (small edge pixels).

Rule:
- Rotate each gate into a vertical blocker; gate cells become bg.
- Tokens drop downward through bg, choosing the extreme column for their side (left→min, right→max).
- Tokens settle at true pipe ends (adjacent to wall/boundary), packing inward.

Key insight: Tokens select drop columns by entry side and land at wall-bounded endpoints, ignoring blocker-bounded ones.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="5792cb4d" lines="44-59">
<description>
Reverse the sequence of colors along the single 4-connected foreground path while keeping the path shape fixed.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- All non-background pixels form one multicolor 4-connected one-pixel-wide path.
- The path has exactly two endpoints and no branches.

Rule:
- Walk the path from one endpoint to the other.
- Replace the colors on those same path cells by the original path colors in reverse order.
- Leave every background pixel unchanged.

Key insight:
- Nothing moves geometrically; only the color string written on the path is reversed between the endpoints.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="eval" puzzle_id="eee78d87" lines="40-55">
<description>
Decode the colored 3x3 contour motif into a 16x16 periodic lattice with the motif stamped in maroon at the center.

Input structure:
- The grid is 6x6 with orange background and one non-orange color.
- The non-orange pixels have a tight 3x3 bbox containing one contour glyph.
- The glyph is one of the task's period-3 contour forms: plus, H, X, or four corners.

Rule:
- Decode the 3x3 glyph into the period-3 orange phase tile.
- Fill a black 16x16 canvas by repeating that orange phase tile.
- Tile the original 3x3 glyph 2x2 and recolor its true pixels to maroon in the centered 6x6 region.

Key insight:
- The colored object is not copied at its input location; it is a small contour code whose phase tells where orange appears in the repeated lattice.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="79cce52d" lines="29-44">
<description>
Crop off the control row and column, then cyclically roll the payload by the two marker offsets.

Input structure
- The grid is square, with a control row on top and a control column on the left.
- The control row and column share one filler color and one single-pixel marker color.
- The lower-right square is the payload and contains none of the control colors.

Rule
- Remove the top control row and left control column.
- Roll the remaining payload down by the marker's zero-based position in the left control column.
- Roll the payload right by the marker's zero-based position in the top control row.

Key insight
- The two border markers are cyclic shift amounts for the payload, not colors to preserve.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="eval" puzzle_id="d59b0160" lines="39-54">
<description>
Delete connected components whose colors match a legend swatch.

Input structure:
- Background (bg) fills most of the grid.
- Top-left corner: a small square legend region bordered on its bottom and right edges by a single color; the interior holds a set of "target" colors.
- Elsewhere: multicolored objects (4-connected components) on the bg.

Rule:
- Read the target color set from inside the legend border.
- For each connected component, check whether it contains **all** target colors.
- Erase (fill with bg) every component that does; leave all others unchanged.
- Output grid is the same size.

Key insight: The legend acts as a filter specification — only components carrying the full color palette shown in the legend are removed.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="train" puzzle_id="84ba50d3" lines="36-54">
<description>
Drop the blue sprites downward relative to the red divider, with vertical sprites passing through to the bottom.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- One non-background color forms a single full-width horizontal divider row.
- The other non-background color forms 4-connected sprites above the divider.

Rule:
- Clear the original sprite pixels.
- A one-column sprite falls to the bottom of its column and deletes the divider
  pixel it crossed.
- Any wider sprite falls until its unique widest horizontal row is immediately
  above the divider row; if the moved sprite overlaps the divider, it covers it.

Key insight:
The red row is not a generic floor: only one-column sprites pass through to the
bottom, while wider sprites are registered by their widest horizontal segment.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

