/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1354760211/10/des.md:176:The arrow encodes both direction and distance: the body length is the trail length, and the arrowhead orientation sets the initial travel direction. The billiard-bounce reflection off grid edges means trails can change direction mid-flight, producing paths that are not simply straight lines -- solvers must simulate the reflection to predict where each trail goes.
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1354760211/10/des.md:194:- Since each shape matches exactly one edge, and top/bottom narrows to a row (2 cells) while left/right narrows to a column (2 cells), the intersection uniquely assigns each shape to one cell: the top-left cell gets the color shared by top-edge and left-edge matches (but since each shape matches only one edge, the cell is determined by the pair of shapes whose edges intersect at that cell -- specifically, each cell is at the intersection of one horizontal-edge color and one vertical-edge color, and receives the shape whose color matches the horizontal edge).
